/*!
    \page style-reference.html
    \title Styles and Style Aware Widgets
    \ingroup qt-gui-concepts
    \brief How styles draw widgets, and how to customize or implement a style.

    Styles (classes that inherit \l{QStyle}) draw on behalf of widgets and
    encapsulate the look and feel of a GUI. \l QStyle is an abstract base class.
    Qt's built-in widgets use it for nearly all of their drawing, so they look
    exactly like the equivalent native widgets.

    Qt comes with a selection of built-in styles. The \c windows and \c fusion
    styles are part of the Qt Widgets module. The platform styles, such as
    \c windows11 on Windows and \c macos on \macos, are plugins that are
    available on their platform only. \l QStyleFactory lists the styles that are
    available at runtime and creates them by name. \l QApplication::setStyle()
    makes a style the application style, and users can select a style with the
    \c -style command-line option.

    A \l QStyle implementation is the way to give a production application its
    own look. \l{Qt Labs StyleKit}{StyleKit} provides one, \l QStyleKitStyle,
    that reads a QML description of the design. For when to use a style,
    StyleKit, or a \l{Qt Style Sheets}{style sheet}, see
    \l{Styling Approaches for Qt Widgets}.

    \section1 Customizing a style

    To change selected aspects of an existing style, inherit \l QProxyStyle and
    reimplement the virtual functions that draw or measure the elements you want
    to change. A proxy style wraps another style and forwards everything you
    don't reimplement to it.

    You can name the base style explicitly or leave it unspecified. An explicit
    base style gives you full control and suits customizations that depend on a
    particular style's behavior. Without a base style, the proxy wraps the
    application style, so your customization applies on top of whatever the
    platform provides.

    \l{QStyle#Creating a Custom Style}{The QStyle class documentation} shows a
    proxy style that replaces the arrows of a spin box.

    \section1 Implementing a style

    \l QCommonStyle provides a convenient base for full style implementations.
    It implements the behavior that all of Qt's styles share, such as the layout
    of subelements and the size calculations, and leaves the drawing to you.
    Inherit \l QCommonStyle and reimplement the drawing functions, together with
    the pixel metrics, subelement rectangles, and style hints that differ from
    the common defaults. Qt's built-in styles aren't part of the public API and
    can't serve as base classes; wrap them with \l QProxyStyle instead.

    Implementing a full style is an involved task. The following pages describe
    how the style API works, trace how a widget is drawn from its paint event to
    the style, and list the elements, states, and options that each widget uses:

    \list
    \li \l{How a Style Draws a Widget} explains style elements, subelements,
        pixel metrics, style hints, style options, the \l QStyle functions, and
        the palette.
    \li \l{Writing Style-Aware Widgets} shows how a custom widget draws through
        the current style, so it looks right in every style.
    \li \l{Styling a Checkbox: A Walkthrough} follows a \l QCheckBox from its
        paint event through \l QCommonStyle and ends with a custom indicator.
    \li \l{Widget Style Reference} lists, for each widget, the style elements it
        draws, the state flags it sets, and the members of its style option.
    \endlist

    The elements the widgets draw are the same in every style, so you can also
    use the reference when you write a style-aware widget or a delegate.

    \section1 Classes for widget styling

    Use these classes to customize an application's appearance.

    \annotatedlist appearance

    \sa QStyle, QProxyStyle, QCommonStyle, QStylePainter, QStyleOption,
        {Styling Approaches for Qt Widgets}
*/
